      "Bamboo viscose top fabric with a smooth, soft hand",
      "Polyurethane laminate lining rather than vinyl",
      "Listings describe it as noiseless",
      "Deep pockets that the listing says fit mattresses up to 18 inches",
      "Machine washable"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Owners commonly report liquid getting through on large spills",
      "Not the pick for incontinence, bed-wetting or pets",
      "Bamboo viscose can lose softness with hot washing"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The nicest-feeling protector here for everyday sweat and minor spills, but not the one to trust with a big accident.",
    "description": "The PlushDeluxe Bamboo protector is for people who want a protector they cannot feel. Its top fabric is viscose made from bamboo, which has a smooth, cool, silky hand that many people prefer to terry, and the listing describes a polyurethane laminate lining under it rather than vinyl. The result is quiet, thin and soft, and it suits memory foam and hybrid beds where a thick protector would dull the feel. The listing says it fits mattresses up to 18 inches deep and is machine washable. It belongs in this guide with a clear caution. While the listing markets it as waterproof, owners commonly report that liquid can soak through on larger spills. That makes it a good protector against nightly sweat, body oils and the odd spilled drink caught quickly, which is what most adults need, but not the one to choose for a child who wets the bed, an older adult with incontinence, or a pet that sleeps on the bed. For those jobs, choose the SafeRest terry protector or an encasement, where the waterproof layer is the main selling point. Wash it in cool water and dry it on low; bamboo viscose tends to lose some softness with very hot washing. If softness and silence matter most and big spills are unlikely, it is a pleasant upgrade."
  },
  {
    "id": "utopia-bedding",
    "name": "Utopia Bedding Waterproof Mattress Protector",
    "category": "Fitted, 200 GSM Terry Top, TPU Waterproof Layer",
    "badge": "Best Budget",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Among the least expensive waterproof protectors on Amazon",
      "200 GSM terry top over a TPU waterproof layer",
      "Dual-sided version waterproofs the long sides as well as the top",
      "Dual-sided version listed for mattresses up to 17 inches deep",
      "Easy to buy several for kids' rooms, guest beds and spares"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Care rules are strict: no bleach, no fabric softener, no ironing",
      "Thinner and less durable than the SafeRest",
      "Size and depth details vary between listings"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The cheapest way to put a real waterproof layer on every bed in the house.",
    "description": "The Utopia Bedding protector is the budget pick and a sensible one for households that need several. It is a fitted protector with a 200 GSM terry top over a TPU waterproof layer, TPU being a thin, flexible polyurethane that is quieter than vinyl. There are two main versions. The standard model waterproofs the top surface only. The dual-sided version, the better buy for children's beds, also waterproofs the two long sides, which catches spills that run off the edge, and its listing says it fits mattresses up to 17 inches deep. Because the price is low, it is easy to buy one for every bed plus a spare, which matters most when a child is potty training or a guest bed gets used only occasionally. The listing's care instructions are worth following closely: wash cold or warm on a gentle cycle, tumble dry low, and avoid bleach, fabric softener and ironing, all of which can break down the waterproof layer. The trade-offs are durability and consistency. It is thinner than the SafeRest, and owners commonly report the waterproof layer wears sooner with frequent washing. Different listings also describe sizes and depths differently, so check the dimensions against your mattress. For a primary bed you want protected for years, the SafeRest is worth the small extra cost."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Mattress Protectors",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-mattress-protector/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best mattress protector?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most beds the best mattress protector is the SafeRest Premium. It pairs a cotton terry top with a waterproof membrane that SafeRest describes as vinyl-free, it goes on like a fitted sheet, and it is easy to wash. For bed bug protection or full coverage, choose a six-sided encasement such as the SureGuard instead."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are waterproof mattress protectors hot?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Modern ones are much cooler than the vinyl covers of the past. Polyurethane and TPU membranes let some moisture vapor through, and thin tops such as polyester or bamboo viscose add little insulation. Very hot sleepers may still notice a difference, and a thin protector on a breathable mattress is the coolest combination."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do mattress protectors make noise?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Protectors with a polyurethane or TPU waterproof layer are usually quiet, and a terry top dampens sound further. The crinkling people remember comes from vinyl and PVC covers, which are stiff and rustle with every movement. Check the listing for the membrane material and avoid anything described as vinyl if noise bothers you."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the difference between a mattress protector and an encasement?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A fitted protector covers the top and sides and goes on like a fitted sheet. An encasement wraps all six sides of the mattress and zips shut. Encasements are the type marketed against bed bugs and offer more complete allergen coverage, while fitted protectors are easier to remove and wash."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do mattress protectors stop bed bugs?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Only a six-sided encasement with a sealed zipper is designed to, and makers such as SureGuard and Malouf market theirs as bed bug proof. Fitted protectors offer no bed bug protection. An encasement does not treat the bed frame or the room, so an active infestation still needs a pest control professional."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How often should you wash a mattress protector?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Wash a fitted protector every one to two months, more often if you sweat heavily, and immediately after any spill. Encasements need washing a few times a year. Use cold or warm water, skip bleach and fabric softener, and dry on low heat, because high heat is what damages the waterproof layer."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a mattress protector go over or under a topper?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Over it. A topper is harder to clean than a sheet, and most foam topper cores cannot be washed at all, so the protector should sit on top to keep sweat and spills away from both the topper and the mattress. Choose one deep enough to cover the combined height."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
