        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            10. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the best mattress protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most beds the best mattress protector is the SafeRest Premium. It pairs a cotton terry top with a waterproof membrane that SafeRest describes as vinyl-free, it goes on like a fitted sheet, and it is easy to wash. For bed bug protection or full coverage, choose a six-sided encasement such as the SureGuard instead.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Are waterproof mattress protectors hot?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Modern ones are much cooler than the vinyl covers of the past. Polyurethane and TPU membranes let some moisture vapor through, and thin tops such as polyester or bamboo viscose add little insulation. Very hot sleepers may still notice a difference, and a thin protector on a breathable mattress is the coolest combination.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mattress protectors make noise?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Protectors with a polyurethane or TPU waterproof layer are usually quiet, and a terry top dampens sound further. The crinkling people remember comes from vinyl and PVC covers, which are stiff and rustle with every movement. Check the listing for the membrane material and avoid anything described as vinyl if noise bothers you.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What is the difference between a mattress protector and an encasement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A fitted protector covers the top and sides and goes on like a fitted sheet. An encasement wraps all six sides of the mattress and zips shut. Encasements are the type marketed against bed bugs and offer more complete allergen coverage, while fitted protectors are easier to remove and wash.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do mattress protectors stop bed bugs?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Only a six-sided encasement with a sealed zipper is designed to, and makers such as SureGuard and Malouf market theirs as bed bug proof. Fitted protectors offer no bed bug protection. An encasement does not treat the bed frame or the room, so an active infestation still needs a pest control professional.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How often should you wash a mattress protector?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Wash a fitted protector every one to two months, more often if you sweat heavily, and immediately after any spill. Encasements need washing a few times a year. Use cold or warm water, skip bleach and fabric softener, and dry on low heat, because high heat is what damages the waterproof layer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a mattress protector go over or under a topper?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Over it. A topper is harder to clean than a sheet, and most foam topper cores cannot be washed at all, so the protector should sit on top to keep sweat and spills away from both the topper and the mattress. Choose one deep enough to cover the combined height.
